cat > KIT-README-NOTE.md <<'EOF'
<!-- Proposed addition for 3d/README.md (kit), under "Composing your own scene" or "API". -->

### Framing a scene for a wide or narrow card

The kit frames each scene by the **short side** of its element (`fraction` × short side),
so a wide, flat scene (`molt`, `wire`) or a small subject on a big base (`crab` on its
sand disc) can end up small in a wide card. To reframe, pass your own `meta.view` to
`createStage` (`target` = the point the camera looks at, `radius` = how much world space
fits at `fraction: 1`), and recompute `fraction` when the card's aspect changes:

```js
import { createStage, SCENES } from './kit/src/index.js';

const def = SCENES.molt;
const meta = { ...def.meta, view: { ...def.meta.view, target: [0.705, -0.07, 0.1] } };
const stage = createStage(el, { create: def.create, meta }, { params: { fraction: 0.9 } });

new ResizeObserver(() => {
  const r = el.getBoundingClientRect();
  // scene is ~5.5 world units wide: fraction ≈ 0.666 × aspect spans ~84% of the width
  if (r.height) stage.set({ fraction: Math.min(1.15, Math.max(0.6, 0.666 * r.width / r.height)) });
}).observe(el);
```

Notes: `fraction` is clamped to 0.3–0.95 in the tunables table but larger values work when
you frame by hand. Pass scene options through `sceneOpts` when you use `createStage`
directly (for example `{ sceneOpts: { pose: 'wave' } }` for `crab`), because only `mount()`
splits them out of the top-level options for you.
EOF
